var e=`# CHAPTER 3 — The Guitar

One day I was walking home after selling some candy on the bus.

And I found something thrown out in the middle of the path.

At the time I didn't really know what it was. But I knew it was an instrument. It was broken — the neck loose, one side cracked. Someone had thrown it away.

I picked it up and took it home.

It didn't even cross my mind to tell Jack. I just thought: cool, I'll take this. No plan at all. I just figured maybe it could be fixed, and if it could be fixed, maybe I could sell it. Ah, my innocence.

I had no idea what a mess I was starting. I only understood after I'd already done it.

---

I fixed it.

Turned out it was a guitar. I bought a set of strings with the candy money — the cheapest one, and even that hurt to pay for. I watched some tutorials online on a borrowed phone, glued the neck, sanded it, adjusted what could be adjusted. It wasn't anything impossible. It was just work, and work I know how to do.

The hard part wasn't the repair. It was the next part.

Then I started trying to play — it was a guitar, damn it.

The first time a clean chord came out — just one, a tiny one — I stood there looking at my own hand like it belonged to someone else. It was a strange feeling. It wasn't pride, it wasn't joy. It was more like: this thing obeyed. I gave an order and it did it.

In my life almost nothing obeys. So that hooked me.

Then I tried to actually play. A song.

And I found out I was terrible.

Not "haven't got the hang of it yet." Terrible. My fingers wouldn't go where they needed to. The sound came out muffled, wrong, ugly. It was much harder than it looked from outside.

Within a week I'd already understood that I was never going to be good at it.

---

But I kept trying.

I can't really explain why. Maybe because back then almost everything in my life was already like that — a hard thing I did because I had to, without liking it, without being good at it. Selling candy at five in the morning. Putting up with Helena. Pretending at school that I belonged there. One bad guitar didn't weigh much on top of that.

Every night, when I got home from work, I sat on the edge of the bed and tried again. With a cloth over the strings so I wouldn't bother anyone. Helena asleep on the other side of the wall, or not even home. She never asked what that muffled noise in my room was. Helena doesn't ask that kind of thing.

There was a week when the tips of my fingers cracked from pressing the strings so much. Bled. I put tape on and kept going. Not because I was determined. I just didn't have the habit of quitting something because of pain — never had that luxury.

And there was one night, around three in the morning, when I almost threw that guitar out the window. I held it up over my head for a while. Then I lowered it, put it back behind the door, and the next day I tried again.

Wrong. Ugly. Every night.

And I didn't tell Jack. That's what matters in this part of the story. I had a guitar, I was learning to play a guitar, and the person who would most want to know that in the whole world had no idea. I kept it that way on purpose. If Jack knew, it would become one of his projects, and I wanted it to be just mine for a little longer.
`;export{e as default};
